xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:22:  **(1a)–(1c) are identities.** On \(Q\)’s jump presentation \((K,\beta_1,\ldots)\), \(u=\beta_j/K\), \(e_j=\gcd(K,\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_j)\),
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:26:  \bar\kappa_F^Q=\frac{K-\beta_j}{e_j}.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:28:  The divisor chain \(e_{j-1}\mid K\) gives \(\gcd(K-\beta_j,e_{j-1})=\gcd(\beta_j,e_{j-1})=e_j\), hence \(\gcd(\bar\kappa_F^Q,\nu_F)=1\) and \(\bar\kappa_F^P=L_0(1-u)=\bar\kappa_F^Q/\nu_F\) with exact denominator \(\nu_F\ge 2\). Replayed on the model’s \(Q\) (\(K=3,\beta_1=1\)) and on a 2–80 single-jump sweep: 0 gcd failures.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:30:  **Statement 3.8 as printed is general.** Verbatim p.14: “For any polynomial \(h(x,y)\), and for any \(F\in V_a\), one has \(D_{h,F}\in\mathbb Z\).” It is not restricted to Keller pairs or to book cells. Notation 3.10 defines \(d_{h,F}\) as the actual leading exponent in a suitable chart; Notation 3.11 sets \(D_{h,F}:=\kappa_F d_{h,F}\). The chart \(y=\phi_{<u}(x)+x^{-u}\eta\) is presentation-independent (shared coefficients below \(u\)). Every exponent of \(h^F\) lies on the group generated by \(L_0^{-1}\mathbb Z\) and \(u\in(\nu_F L_0)^{-1}\mathbb Z\), hence \(d_{h,F}\in(\nu_F L_0)^{-1}\mathbb Z\). Q makes \(D_{h,F}\) integral; P gives \(D_{h,F}\in\nu_F^{-1}\mathbb Z\), not integral in general.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:32:  **Explicit witness, independently recomputed.** Section 3’s \(f=(xy^2-1)(xy^3-1)(x^2y^3+1)\), \(a=0\), vertex \(F=I_Q(1/3)\in V_{1,0}\) (so Statement 3.8 applies). Exact substitution \(x=t^3\), \(y=\eta/t\):
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:33:  \(f^F\) has \(t\)-exponents \(\{0,1,3,4\}\), leading term \(t^4\eta^5(\eta^3-1)\), so \(d_{f,F}=4/3\). Along \(P:y=x^{-1/2}\) one has \(\alpha_0=0\le 1/3<\alpha_1=1/2\), hence \(\kappa_F(P)=2/e_0=1\). Therefore
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:39:  **Proposition 5.5 independently forces Q at poles.** Printed p.26 boxed (18): \(\Lambda(P)=D_{g,F}/\nu_F\) if \(c=0\), and \(=D_{g,F}\) if \(c\neq 0\). Printed \(c=0\) proof (p.27): \(P\) has \(\alpha_m<u\); any other \(Q\) realizing \(F\) has characteristics \((\nu_F\kappa,\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_m,u)\); then \(\Lambda(P)=\kappa d_{g,F}=D_F/\nu_F\). Together with \(D_{g,F}=\kappa_F d_{g,F}\) this is \(\kappa_F=\nu_F\kappa\), the Q-value. P-value \(\kappa_F=\kappa\) would make the displayed equality \(\kappa d=(\kappa d)/\nu_F\), false at a pole. The dropped \(g\)-subscripts are the already audited Proposition 5.5 typo; boxed (18) carries the correct subscript. Scope is exactly the in-context \(c=0\) pole case the note claims — it does **not** by itself force Q at a nonpole class-C merge. The note already records that caveat.
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:103:  | 9.6 p.53 | pattern \(p=\eta^2(\eta^\nu-c_1^\nu)\cdots\), \(q=\eta(\eta^\nu-c_1^\nu)\cdots\); Prop 8.1(v) \(\Rightarrow M_F=1\) | no |
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:113:  Branches at \(x=\infty\): \(P:y=x^{-1/2}\) (\(\kappa=2,\beta_1=1,\alpha_1=1/2\)), \(Q:y=x^{-1/3}\) (\(\kappa=3,\beta_1=1,\alpha_1=1/3\)), \(R:y=\zeta x^{-2/3}\) (\(\kappa=3,\beta_1=2,\alpha_1=2/3\)). Contacts: \(O(P,Q)=1/3\), \(O(Q,R)=1/3\), \(O(P,R)=1/2\) (first differing exponent). Hence \(F=I_P(1/3)=I_Q(1/3)=I_R(1/3)\in V_{1,0}\cap V_{2,0}\) and \(G=I_P(1/2)=I_R(1/2)\). Along \(P\) in the \(F\)-chart, \(\eta=x^{1/6}\to 0\), so the zero direction out of \(F\) is \(P\) and reaches \(G\). Data:
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:119:  **Charts, Statement 3.17, down-arrows.** Exact substitution (Finding 1): \(d_F=4/3\), \(p_F=\eta^5(\eta^3-1)\), \(\deg=8\), \(\mathrm{mult}(p_F,0)=5\); \(d_G=1/2\), \(f_G^+=-x^{1/2}\eta^3(\eta^2-1)\), \(\deg p_G=5\). Then \(4/3=1/2+(1/2-1/3)\cdot 5\) and \(\mathrm{mult}(p_F,0)=\deg p_G\), which is 3.17 in 9.3 letters. Down-arrows: \(0<4/3<(2/3)\cdot 8=16/3\) and \(0<1/2<(1/2)\cdot 5\).
xmodel/grok-h5a-review.md:121:  **Isolated 8.1(iv).** Printed p.40: \(\delta p q'-(1-u)p'q=p\). Direct differentiation on the displayed \(p=\eta^5(\eta^3-1)\), \(q=\eta(\eta^3-1)\) gives leftover \(2p\), exactly as (15) prints. The printed normalization is leftover \(p\); \(q\mapsto q/2\) produces it (and \(h\mapsto h/2\) realizes the scaled top). Nonzero roots of \(p_F\) are simple, \(p_F\) is not a proper power (\(\gcd(8,5)=1\)), so \(p^i=p_F\) forces \(i=1\). Printed (f) with \(i=1\) then reads \(8/4=(3\cdot 1+5)/(3\cdot 1+1)\). The isolated ODE therefore does not force equal indices. The pair is **not** the Proposition 4.2 approximate-root tower of a Keller pair; the note says this.
